-docker-compose-up           (not found)\n47:   → finds pos-docker-compose              → runs with args \"up jellyfin\"\n48: ```\n49: \n50: All non-interactive commands log to `~/.local/share/linux_post_install/logs/`.\n51: \n52: ### Shared Library (`lib/common.sh`)\n53: \n54: Sourced by most scripts. Key functions:\n55: \n56: | Function | Purpose |\n57: |----------|---------|\n58: | `log \"msg\"` | Green `[+]` status message |\n59: | `warn \"msg\"` | Yellow `[!]` warning |\n60: | `err \"msg\"` | Red `ERROR:` + exit 1 |\n61: | `ok \"msg\"` | Green `OK` prefix |\n62: | `section \"title\"` | Cyan-bordered section header |\n63: | `step N T \"msg\"` | Numbered step header |\n64: | `run cmd` | Executes command, respects `$DRY_RUN` |\n65: | `spawn \"msg\" cmd` | Animated braille spinner + elapsed time |\n66: | `timer_start` / `timer_stop` | Elapsed time tracking |\n67: | `confirm \"prompt\"` | y/N prompt with optional default |\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## Adding a New CLI Tool\n72: \n73: ### 1. Create the script\n74: \n75: ```bash\n76: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n77: \n78: set -euo pipefail\n79: \n80: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib/common.sh\"\n81: \n82: usage() {\n83:     cat <<EOF\n84: Usage: my-tool <argument>\n85: EOF\n86:     exit 0\n87: }\n88: \n89: case \"${1:-}\" in\n90:     -h|--help|\"\") usage ;;\n91: esac\n92: \n93: # --- script logic ---\n94: ```\n95: \n96: **Conventions:**\n97: - Shebang: `#!/usr/bin/env bash`\n98: - Strict mode: `set -euo pipefail`\n99: - `--help` flag: accept `-h` / `--help` via `case` pattern\n100: - Shared library: always source `common.sh` for colors, logging, spinners\n101: - Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error\n102: - No shared lib? Inline fallbacks:\n103:   ```bash\n104:   log()  { echo \"[+] $*\"; }\n105:   warn() { echo \"[!] $*\"; }\n106:   err()  { echo \"ERROR: $*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n107:   ```\n108: \n109: ### 2. Add system dependencies\n110: \n111: Add package names to the `PACKAGES` array in `preinstall.sh`:\n112: \n113: ```bash\n114: PACKAGES=(\n115:     ...\n116:     your-package\n117: )\n118: ```\n119: \n120: ### 3. Add config files (if needed)\n121: \n122: Place defaults in `config/` and add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`. If they contain secrets, add to `.gitignore` and document in README.\n123: \n124: ### 4. Add SSH keys (if needed)\n125: \n126: Place public keys in `config/authorized_keys` (one per line). `postinstall.sh` reads this file automatically.\n127: \n128: ### 5. Update README\n129: \n130: Add a section under the relevant category in README.md.\n131: \n132: ### 6. Test\n133: \n134: ```bash\n135: bash -n bin/your-tool\n136: shellcheck bin/your-tool\n137: ./bin/your-tool --help\n138: ```\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## Adding an Optional App\n143: \n144: ### 1. Create the installer\n145: \n146: ```bash\n147: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n148: set -euo pipefail\n149: source \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../../lib/common.sh\"\n150: \n151: install_myapp() {\n152:     command -v myapp &>/dev/null && { log \"myapp already installed\"; return 0; }\n153:     spawn \"Installing myapp\" sudo apt install -y myapp\n154: }\n155: \n156: install_myapp\n157: ```\n158: \n159: Place it in `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`. It auto-appears in the picker — no registration needed.\n160: \n161: **Categories:** `browsers`, `development`, `media`, `networking`, `remote-access`, `system`, `utilities`\n162: \n163: ### 2. Conventions\n164: \n165: - Idempotent: check `command -v` before installing\n166: - APT packages → `sudo apt install -y` inside `spawn`\n167: - Official scripts → `curl ... | sh` inside `spawn`\n168: - Flatpak → `flatpak install -y flathub <app-id>` inside `spawn`\n169: - `.deb` files → download to temp, `sudo apt install -y ./file.deb` inside `spawn`\n170: - `usermod` for groups → print re-login reminder\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## Editing an Existing Tool\n175: \n176: 1. Find the script in `bin/`\n177: 2. Understand its contract (args, output, exit codes)\n178: 3. Make the change — keep it idempotent\n179: 4. Update README if behaviour changed\n180: 5. Run `shellcheck` on the modified file\n181: \n182: ---\n183: \n184: ## Best Practices\n185: \n186: ### Idempotency\n187: \n188: Check before creating, use `>>` with grep guards, don't overwrite user configs.\n189: \n190: ### Error Handling\n191: \n192: ```bash\n193: set -euo pipefail\n194: command -v docker &>/dev/null || { echo \"docker not found\"; exit 1; }\n195: [[ -n \"${1:-}\" ]] || { echo \"Usage: my-tool <arg>\"; exit 1; }\n196: ```\n197: \n198: ### Portability\n199: \n200: Targets **Debian** and **Ubuntu**. Use `apt`, assume bash at `/usr/bin/env bash`, check tools with `command -v`.\n201: \n202: ### Dry-run Support\n203: \n204: Scripts support `--dry-run`. Use the `run()` helper:\n205: \n206: ```bash\n207: run() {\n208:     if [ \"$DRY_RUN\" -eq 1 ]; then\n209:         log \"(dry-run) $*\"\n210:     else\n211:         \"$@\"\n212:     fi\n213: }\n214: run sudo apt install -y git\n215: ```\n216: \n217: ### Security\n218: \n219: - Never hardcode secrets — put them in `config/` (gitignored)\n220: - `chmod 600` for sensitive files\n221: - Validate input before shell commands\n222: - Use `sudo` only where needed\n223: \n224: ### Naming\n225: \n226: - CLI tools: `bin/pos-<category>-<command>`\n227: - Legacy wrappers: `bin/wr-*`\n228: - App installers: `apps/<category>/<name>.sh`\n229: - Lowercase with hyphens\n230: \n231: ---\n232: \n233: ## Working with Systemd\n234: \n235: Create `systemd/<name>.service` — `postinstall.sh` copies it to `/etc/systemd/system/` and enables it automatically.\n236: \n237: ```ini\n238: [Unit]\n239: Description=My Service\n240: After=network-online.target\n241: Wants=network-online.target\n242: \n243: [Service]\n244: Type=simple\n245: ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/your-script.sh\n246: Restart=on-failure\n247: RestartSec=10\n248: \n249: [Install]\n250: WantedBy=multi-user.target\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ---\n254: \n255: ## Working with Config Files\n256: \n257: 1. Place the file in `config/`\n258: 2. Add copy logic to `postinstall.sh`:\n259: \n260: ```bash\n261: if [ -f config/your-config.conf ]; then\n262:     mkdir -p \"$HOME/.config/your-app\"\n263:     cp config/your-config.conf \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n264:     chmod 600 \"$HOME/.config/your-app/your-config.conf\"\n265: fi\n266: ```\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## Docker Compose / ScaleTail\n271: \n272: The installer clones [ScaleTail](https://github.com/tailscale-dev/ScaleTail) templates to `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/` — 119+ self-hosted services with a Tailscale sidecar pattern. Each service gets a `tail-xxxxx.ts.net` URL via `network_mode: service:tailscale`.\n273: \n274: ### Config Strategy — Three Layers\n275: \n276: Values cascade from least to most specific:\n277: \n278: ```\n279: Template .env          (per-service defaults from ScaleTail)\n280:        ↓\n281: Global config          (~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env)\n282:        ↓\n283: Per-service .env       (/srv/<service>/.env) — created on first deploy, NEVER overwritten\n284: ```\n285: \n286: On first `pos docker compose up <service>`:\n287: 1. Template `.env` is copied to `/srv/<service>/.env`\n288: 2. Matching keys from global config are filled in\n289: 3. If `TS_AUTHKEY` is still empty, you're prompted to enter it\n290: 4. After that, the per-service `.env` is **never touched** — not even by `update`\n291: \n292: ### Layout\n293: \n294: | Path | Purpose | Mutability |\n295: |------|---------|------------|\n296: | `/usr/local/share/linux_post_install/scale-tail/services/<name>/` | ScaleTail templates (git repo) | Read-only |\n297: | `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` | Your global defaults | Edit via `config set` or `config edit` |\n298: | `/srv/<service>/` | Active deployment | Per-service `.env` preserved forever |\n299: \n300: ### Key Commands\n301: \n302: | Command | Behaviour |\n303: |---------|-----------|\n304: | `pos docker compose up <service>` | Deploys to `$SERVICES_BASE/<service>/`, creates `config/` + `data/`, generates `.env` from global defaults |\n305: | `pos docker compose down <service>` | Stops the stack |\n306: | `pos docker compose update` | `git pull` templates + refreshes `compose.yaml` for all deployed services (`.env` untouched) |\n307: | `pos docker compose config set K=V` | Sets a global default in `~/.config/linux_post_install/compose.env` |\n308: | `pos docker compose config show` | Displays current global config and `SERVICES_BASE` |\n309: | `pos docker compose config edit` | Opens global config in `$EDITOR` |\n310: \n311: ### Global Config Keys\n312: \n313: | Key | Required | Default | Purpose |\n314: |-----|----------|---------|---------|\n315: | `TS_AUTHKEY` | Yes | — | Tailscale auth key for sidecar networking |\n316: | `TZ` | No | `Europe/Amsterdam` | Timezone for services |\n317: | `DNS_SERVER` | No | `9.9.9.9` | Custom DNS server |\n318: | `SERVICES_BASE` | No | `/srv` | Root directory for all deployments |\n319: \n320: ---\n321: \n322: ## Commit Guidelines\n323: \n324: - Use conventional prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`\n325: - Explain *why*, not just *what*\n326: - One logical change per commit\n327: \n328: ```\n329: feat: add pos-disk-usage for monitoring disk space\n330: fix: pos-network-ip fails when no default route exists\n331: docs: add example output for pos-network-scan\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## Useful Commands\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check a single script\n340: bash -n bin/my-script\n341: \n342: # ShellCheck linting\n343: shellcheck bin/my-script\n344: \n345: # Check all scripts\n346: for f in bin/* apps/*/*.sh lib/common.sh install.sh preinstall.sh postinstall.sh; do\n347:     bash -n \"$f\" || echo \"FAIL: $f\"\n348: done\n349: \n350: # Init submodule\n351: git submodule update --init\n352: \n353: # Pull latest ScaleTail templates\n354: git submodule update --remote compose/scale-tail\n355: \n356: # Test install in Docker\n357: docker run --rm -it -v $PWD:/repo ubuntu:22.04 bash\n358: # inside: cd /repo && ./install.sh\n359: \n360: # Test app installers\n361: ./apps/install.sh\n362: ./apps/install.sh --all\n363: ./apps/install.sh docker vscode\n364: ```\n\n(End of file - total 364 lines)\n</content>",
